when putin sent tanks into ukraine he  started a war that quickly became much  bigger than just an invasion  everywhere you look all sorts of  relationships are changing between  countries  between economies  and between people and their governments  our correspondents around the globe  report on how this attack is having  consequences for countries and people  far from the battlefield  but let's start in ukraine itself  this war is a fight for ukraine's  survival as an independent state one  which will answer the question of where  its future lies the majority of  ukrainians have solved this question a  long time ago they want to be aligned  with the west but the western partners  especially in europe had doubts about  this they were debating this question  much to the frustration of many  ukrainians further and further the  resolve that the ukrainians have shown  in this war has changed this and now  ukraine's ambitions to be part of the  west are recognized by many partner  countries the most notable sign of this  shift in western perception was the  beginning of the membership process by  the european union something that  ukraine has long asked for but that was  always rejected by europe on different  grounds  so in the end this war has not only  brought ukrainians closer together it  has not only united the country and has  also diluted doubts about each other  about the western ukrainians about the  eastern ukrainians and the eastern  ukrainians about the western ukrainians  it has also brought ukraine and the west  closer together  the flip side to this a massive divide  between the west and russia  internationally russia is now more  isolated than ever at least in the west  and this divide between russia and the  west is here to stay not just for weeks  or months but for many many years russia  has become a pariah state an outcast  this had led moscow on the increasingly  desperate search for new partners  elsewhere be it selling more oil to  china or servicing western built planes  with the help of iran iran is  interesting because it's a country that  has been internationally sanctions for  decades and learned to live with and  work around the sanctions and also the  baltic states are pushing back against  moscow latvia lithuania and estonia have  clamped down on immigration from russia  and are striving for energy independence  here in latvia where i am now there is  even talk of tearing down all soviet  monuments from the past the iron curtain  is back like it was in the soviet union  if that was one of putin's goals the  restoration of the soviet union he  successfully achieved that goal at least  as far as the cold war is concerned  the war has highlighted ukraine's vital  role in feeding the world tens of  millions of tons of grain have been  stuck in storage due to the fighting  people in many places are feeling the  pressure africa may be thousands of  miles away from the war in ukraine but  dining tables here are taking a hit  nigeria imports more than 80 percent of  its wheat and almost half of the food  served in nigerian homes are derived  from wheat things like bread noodles  pasta and cereal many nigerians who were  already dealing with inflation after the  kobe 19 lockdown are facing the threat  of hunger and even starvation the rising  cost of food and increasing poverty has  worsened the problem of insecurity all  across the country crimes like  kidnapping for ransom are on the rise  and things are projected to get worse if  nigerians continue to get more  dissatisfied with the government over  its failure to get a grip of the  economic and security situation the  president faces the threat of being  impeached before the general elections  in 2023 nigeria has the biggest  population in africa political  instability here could spread beyond its  borders to the rest of west africa the  diplomatic fallout has also been global  india has long been friends with russia  to get weapons the west won't sell and  as a buffer against major rival china  india is re-examining this because of  how the west sees such a friendship and  because things between russia and china  are shifting  despite india's proximity to russia  successive indian governments have been  warming to the united states they see  washington's a key partner at many  levels  this war in ukraine has exposed what  some see as india's strategic ambiguity  despite initial fumbling india seems to  have found its balance on the diplomatic  tie probe when it discovered the western  world was not keen to push india into a  diplomatic corner at this very sensitive  time india gained confidence the foreign  ministry began talking about balancing  india's interests and it reacted sharply  at what it perceived as western double  standards especially when it came to  importing russian oil but all this could  backfire because india's refusal to  condemn russia's invasion of ukraine  could weaken its mantra of territorial  integrity and sovereignty especially  when it comes to china the two countries  are rivals and neighbors they share a  long border they have territorial  disputes and they have a fraught  relationship  in this context if russia and china come  closer together it could make it very  difficult for india and it could once  again start wobbling on the diplomatic  tightrope  in the midst of the ukrainian war  india's initial shaky position  stabilized and even gained traction it  was envied by countries who could not  afford to choose one side over the other  and india is a part of many multilateral  groups some overlapping and some not  this increased its diplomatic space to  maneuver and also enhanced its sense of  importance  not far away china carries out  unprecedented military drills in the  taiwan strait putting taipei on edge  they were portrayed as a reaction to  america's top parliamentarian nancy  pelosi visiting the democratic island at  the beginning of august  taiwan held defense drills of its own  with an eye on parallels between itself  and ukraine  taiwan's ministry of national defense  said this year's exercise is based on  war game simulations that are modeled  after lessons learned from the russia  ukraine war it includes scenarios like  fighter jets intercepting invading  warplanes and also  helicopters playing cat and mouse with  submarines taiwanese president tain once  said these exercises reflect the ability  and determination that taiwan can defend  itself  and this is also the first time that the  ministry of national defense has called  on reservists to join the exercises  highlighting the importance of ensuring  taiwan's combat readiness as the war in  ukraine continues  one of the biggest changes that has  taken place here in taiwan over the last  few months has been taiwanese people's  growing awareness of the risks of a  potential military conflict with china  and their willingness to participate in  first aid and combat related training  to a place now where asia and europe  meet  turkey is playing a complex role in the  crisis one that could become harder to  manage in the months ahead the war has  put turkey in a difficult position as a  member of nato the country is  strategically aligned with the west but  president recep tayyip erdogan and his  government are performing a delicate  balancing act supporting ukraine while  trying not to antagonize russia turkey  has provided kiev with combat drones but  resisted joining western sanctions  against moscow and the turkish leader is  trying to turn the challenge into an  opportunity casting his country as the  most engaged mediator between the two  sides erdogan facilitated the first  attempts at peace talks and played a key  role in securing the grain deal signed  by russia and ukraine in july aimed at  averting a global food crisis and he is  using this center stage position to  project greater turkish influence in the  broader region but also internationally  vis-a-vis the west but as the war drags  on and with an election coming up here  in turkey in 2023  observers are asking how long erdogan  can continue his balancing act or  whether he will have to pick a side  the west led by the u.s is now having to  face in two directions at once  plans to make china the primary focus  for geopolitical attention have had to  be adapted  that's because russia has once again  emerged as the west's rival and folk  raising the specter of the cold war the  us sees russia as a dangerous agitator  with nefarious aims to upend western  democracy and reshape the global  security order there's been a shift in  rhetoric and tone  there was much made of washington's  pivot to asia during the obama  administration during the trump  administration china emerged as a key  adversary china certainly hasn't  disappeared from the picture but  attention has been jolted back to europe  the war has made one thing pretty clear  the u.s still provides the backbone of  european security all of those previous  discussions about european strategic  autonomy have proven to be far too early  the white house is increasingly facing  what it deems to be major security  threats on two fronts china and russia  two foes forging a deepening strategic  alliance according to nato the closer  beijing and moscow got the more the u.s  will be looking to its european partners  to forge an alliance of democracies  for europe the big deal is energy  europeans need russian gas and yet  paying for it meant they were funding an  economy that they were also trying to  punish with sanctions  russia has been europe's biggest  supplier of energy and for decades  member countries of the european union  could rely on cheap russian gas imports  to heat homes and to power their  industry  as part of the sanctions the eu imposed  on moscow for its aggression on ukraine  the bloc began weaning itself off of  russian oil coal and gas ending decades  of dependency on russian fossil fuels  the war in ukraine is testing unity as  spheres grow that russia which has  already been restricting gas supply will  completely turn off the taps eu member  countries took the decision  to reduce their consumption so as to  save to have enough for the winter but  some member countries feel that it's  unfair that everybody's been asked to  reduce by the same amount even though  not everybody uses the same amount of  russian gas  russia was once considered a strategic  partner it's now been designated the  most significant threat to peace and  stability in the euro area  that's led to finland and sweden taking  the historic decision to join nato  for their part nato allies have  committed to increasing their defence  spending and the alliance has bolstered  its presence in europe increasing the  number of troops  the economic fallout of the war is  making life harder everywhere  it's combined with the uneven recovery  from the corona pandemic to produce  inflation worse than has been seen in  decades even before russia's invasion  prices had already been climbing the  pandemic had scrambled supply chains  leading to shortages and then there was  an energy crunch energy producers scaled  back production when lockdowns depressed  demand but then the global economy  bounced back with a vengeance and energy  demand and energy prices did the same  together ukraine and russia produce  about a fourth of global wheat supplies  but the war made getting grain out of  the so-called fred basket of the world  difficult even though deals have since  been struck to change that it has  however put a strain on many countries  dependent on those imports and made food  around the world harder to afford  central banks around the world are  trying to cool inflation by raising  interest rates the idea is that by  making money more expensive to borrow  people will buy and spend less and that  will dial back demand and help prices  climb back down  theoretically anyway but that also means  slowing down and already slowing economy  even further  and maybe even tipping it into recession  there's also a limit to what central  banks can do they cannot grow wheat they  cannot produce energy and they cannot  stop the war and as further developments  in that war remain uncertain so too does  the outlook for the global economy  uncertainty  complexity  instability  division  six months on the ukraine war is  cascading through human lives everywhere  the unwelcome effects will likely make  things harder and more dangerous long  after the war ends  you
 

